# SYSVOL replication settings. Sourced by every script in this directory,
# after ../lab.env. Edit here only.

# Where SYSVOL lives inside a DC container.
SYSVOL_PATH="/var/lib/samba/sysvol"

# Transport. The lab uses an ephemeral rsync daemon on the source DC because
# the containers have no sshd. In production this would be "ssh" and the pull
# command becomes  rsync -aXH --delete -e ssh root@<pdc>:/var/lib/samba/sysvol/
# The rsync FLAGS are identical either way; only the transport differs.
SYSVOL_TRANSPORT="rsyncd"
RSYNC_PORT=8730
RSYNC_MODULE="SysVol"
RSYNC_PIDFILE="/run/rsyncd-sysvol.pid"
RSYNC_CONF="/run/rsyncd-sysvol.conf"

# Only the lab subnet may pull SYSVOL.
RSYNC_HOSTS_ALLOW="${NET_PREFIX:-172.15.4}.0/24"

# rsync flags.
#
#   -rlptD  content, symlinks, permission bits, mtimes, devices/specials.
#           This is `-a` MINUS `-o` and `-g` -- see below.
#   -X      xattrs. THIS is what carries `security.NTACL`, the SID-based NT
#           ACL that Samba's acl_xattr VFS module treats as authoritative.
#           Without -X a freshly synced target has no NT ACL at all and
#           `samba-tool ntacl sysvolcheck` fails with ENODATA
#           ("[Errno 61] No data available").
#   -H      hardlinks. Cheap insurance; SYSVOL normally has none.
#
# Deliberately ABSENT: -A / --acls, and the -o/-g of -a.
#
#   POSIX ACLs and file ownership on SYSVOL are expressed as NUMERIC uids and
#   gids from Samba's idmap.ldb (the 3000000+ xid range). idmap.ldb is LOCAL
#   to each DC and is NOT replicated by DRS, so the same number denotes a
#   DIFFERENT SID on every DC. Copying them verbatim silently regrants the
#   ACL to whoever holds that number locally -- in this lab, xid 3000002 is
#   SYSTEM on dc1 and BUILTIN\Guests on dc2. See README.md, "The idmap trap".
#
#   `samba-tool ntacl sysvolreset` on the target re-derives ownership and the
#   POSIX ACLs from the SIDs using the LOCAL idmap. That is the step that
#   makes this correct, and sysvol-replicate.sh always runs it.
#   --delete-after  rather than plain --delete: deletions happen only after the
#                   whole transfer succeeded, so an interrupted run cannot
#                   leave a DC with a half-emptied SYSVOL. This is the form the
#                   Samba wiki uses.
#   --exclude=DfsrPrivate
#                   a Windows DFS-R staging directory. Samba never creates one,
#                   and no Samba document recommends this exclude. It is here
#                   purely so that pointing the source at a Windows DC (the
#                   robocopy migration case) cannot drag DFS-R internals in.
RSYNC_FLAGS=(-rlptD -X -H --delete-after --exclude=DfsrPrivate)

# Upstream's alternative to dropping --acls: make the xids agree everywhere by
# copying idmap.ldb from the PDC emulator to every other DC. See
# sysvol-idmap-sync.sh and README.md. Off by default because it overwrites a
# live local database on four DCs; the sysvolreset path needs no such surgery.
SYNC_IDMAP="no"

# Lock file so an overlapping timer run cannot interleave with a live sync.
SYSVOL_LOCK="/run/sysvol-replicate.lock"
